  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday: Current fog and low stratus sits north and \neast of a line from about Wibaux to Wolf Point to Malta late this \nmorning and is expected to clear/breakup slightly through the \nafternoon from SW to NE. However, model data is highly suggestive\nthat once sun goes down the low stratus line will fill back in \nand progress southwest again. There are a couple locations that \nindicate 1/4 mile for an hour or two such as Daniels, Sheridan,\nand central Phillips county and other higher elevation \nlocation's, but this does not appear to be widespread or long \nlasting enough in the data sets to warrant a dense fog advisory... \nYET. And confidence in placement of quarter mile fog is low, with \na lack of CAMs data. Complicating this whole process further \ntonight is overcast high Chinook arch clouds, which are not \nmodeled by most data sets and will block satellite view of surface\ncoverage of the Fog's extent during this time.\n\nFriday night through Monday: An upper-level ridge over Montana \nwill result in dry conditions and temperatures 10 to 15 degrees \nabove seasonal averages persisting though Monday.\n\nMonday night onward: Model ensembles begin to show discrepancies \nfrom monday night onward. However newer runs are beginning to \nre-phase back into a more SW flow pattern with cooler and \npotentially wetter weather hear onward.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nSignificant deviation from hourly NBM for Visibility tonight \nwhich was quite pathetic in terms of FOG prediction. Used a blend \nof RRFS, HRRR, and minimal GLAMP to attempt to improve placement \nevery 3rd hour and interpolated hourly data between every 3rd hour\nof what data is left after the HRRR dropped out at hour 12-15 for\nsome semblance of reality, now that most CAMs are gone.\n \n- Confidence in fair conditions persisting through Monday is high\n  (90%).\n\nDON/GAH",
